//
// A slot written by a neighboring compute tile's own DMA, not the host --
// test/npu-xrt/tile_sourced_ctrl_pkt_spike/aie.mlir's mechanism, at a real,
// near-maximum-size (0x1FF0 byte) slot instead of a 2-word proof, built with
// the `aie.iron.overlay` API's `source=` transport. Every control-packet
// chunk is sent through one reused, self-looping `aie.dma_bd` -- not one
// static BD per chunk, so this is no longer capped at a source tile's
// 16-entry BD table. See iron_api_tile_sourced.py and
// ProgramMemorySlot._load_tile_sourced's comments.
//
// Three phases (three `--tags`) exercise multi-phase tile-sourced scheduling:
// `slot.load()` called once per phase from the source's own core_fn, paced by
// `_ensure_ack_rig`'s reverse DMA channel so an overlay is not written until
// the target core has actually finished with the previous one.
//
// Three rather than two on purpose. The ack rig puts a second TileDma on the
// source tile, and BD ids used to be numbered per DMA region instead of per
// tile, so its BDs collided with the forward transport's and every
// multi-phase design failed on hardware close to 100% of the time. Two phases
// is one ack round-trip and happened to survive; the third is what actually
// exercised the collision, so that is what guards the fix here.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
//
// RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
// RUN: %python %S/../iron_api_tile_sourced.py --tags 5,7,9 --out %t
//
// RUN: %host_clang %S/../host.cpp -o %t/host.exe -std=c++17 -Wall %xrt_flags %host_link_flags %test_utils_flags
// RUN: %t/host.exe -x %t/aie.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i %t/insts.bin --tags 5,7,9 --n-elems 4 | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: phase 0: overlay 5 ok
// CHECK: phase 1: overlay 7 ok
// CHECK: phase 2: overlay 9 ok
// CHECK: PASS!
